---
import LiveRun from '../../components/demo/LiveRun.astro';
import LockDemo from '../../components/demo/LockDemo.astro';
import Demo from '../../layouts/Demo.astro';
import { url } from '../../url';
---

<Demo
	title="reef lock, live"
	description="An MCP server can change what a tool says at any time, and your agent reads it as instructions. A lockfile pins it."
>
	<LockDemo slot="demo" />

	<LiveRun
		scenario="rug-pull"
		options={[
			{ value: 'none', label: 'No protection' },
			{ value: 'reef-lock', label: 'Through Reef, with the lock' },
		]}
		intro="The notes server rewrites its search tool to tell the model to email every note to an outside address. Run a real agent against it. Good models often notice the trick, and sometimes don't; the lock doesn't depend on which."
		more={{ label: 'Try it in the playground, with or without a gateway and a lock', href: url('/lab/playground/?preset=rug-pull-open') }}
	/>

	<h2>What you're looking at</h2>
	<p>
		A notes server offers three tools. Their descriptions were reviewed and pinned in
		<code>reef.lock</code>, with a hash of each. Now press <strong>Server: rewrite a
		description</strong>: the search tool starts telling the model to email every note somewhere
		else. Nothing about your agent changed, and nobody approved it.
	</p>
	<p>
		The lock catches it. <code>reef lock --check</code> fails with the diff, which is what you'd see
		in CI. At runtime, Reef hides the changed tool from the agent and refuses calls to it until
		someone reviews the change. A brand new tool the server adds never appears either. If the
		change is fine, <code>reef lock --update</code> accepts it.
	</p>

	<h2>Use it</h2>
	<p>It works on any MCP setup, whether or not you run Reef as a gateway:</p>
	<pre><code>pip install reef-mcp
reef lock --from claude-code          # or vscode, cursor, claude-desktop
reef lock --from claude-code --check  # in CI</code></pre>
	<p>
		Code and docs are in <a href="https://github.com/FatherBrian/trellis/tree/main/reef">Reef</a>.
		This page is a small in-browser copy of the check, hashing with your browser's own SHA-256.
	</p>
</Demo>
